<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pasquale Paoli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1807">1807</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pasquale Paoli was a Corsican patriot elected general of the nation in 1755. He governed the island as an independent republic under a written constitution admired across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and defeated him at Ponte Novu in 1769. Returning during the Revolution, he broke with France and created the Anglo-Corsican Kingdom in 1794, dying in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli.jpg" alt="Pasquale Paoli" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William BEECHEY · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_de_Pascal_Paoli_-_William_BEECHEY.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pasquale Paoli was born in the hamlet of Stretta in Morosaglia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, the second son of the physician and patriot Giacinto Paoli, one of three Generals of the People in the revolt against Genoese rule. The Corsicans regarded the Genoese as failing in government, citing the high murder rate from vendetta, raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates, oppressive taxes and economic depression. After the rebellion of 1729 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> called in foreign troops. When the Corsicans surrendered to the French in 1739, Giacinto went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> with his fourteen-year-old son, who received a classical education there and in 1741 joined the Corsican regiment of the royal Neapolitan army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Corsican exiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> were seeking a skilled general. Pasquale opposed a plan to ask the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a> to take command and proposed a native Corsican government instead, and a popular election called at Caccia made him General-in-Chief. Only the highland clans voted, and the lowlanders chose Mario Matra, who was soon killed in battle. Paoli confined the Genoese to their citadels, and in November 1755 the people ratified his constitution, which proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> sovereign and independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and is described as the first written under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> principles. He founded a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Corte</a>, the capital, in 1765, and wished Italian to be the official language.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in May 1768, and France began its conquest that September. Paoli fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war from the mountains but was defeated at Ponte Novu in 1769 and took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he joined the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Samuel Johnson</a>, James Boswell recorded his account in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-account-of-corsica">An Account of Corsica</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> gave him a pension.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, Paoli returned in 1790 under an amnesty and was elected unanimously president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>. He broke with the Revolution over the execution of the king, and in 1793 a consulta at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Corte</a> seceded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and asked for British protection. The Bonapartes, who had denounced him as a traitor, were driven from the island. A British fleet under Admiral Samuel Hood arrived in 1794, and the Anglo-Corsican Kingdom lasted until 1796 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> as sovereign head of state, though the relationship was never clearly defined. The Crown invited Paoli to resign, and he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in October 1795. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 5 February 1807; his bones were brought back to Corsica in 1889.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pasquale Paoli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The long Corsican revolt from 1729, including the rule of <b>Pasquale Paoli</b> from 1755, led Genoa to cede the island to France by the Treaty of Versailles in 1768.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pasquale Paoli</b>’s Corsican Republic (1755–1769) is thought to have had the first constitution in the world to give women the vote, to female heads of family; Rousseau wrote that “some day that little island will astonish Europe”, and five American counties are named Paoli.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pasquale Paoli</b> returned to Corsica in 1790 but broke with the French Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/anglo-corsican-kingdom">Anglo-Corsican Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After decades of rebellion against Genoa, the Corsican assembly elected <b>Pasquale Paoli</b> as General of the Nation in July 1755.</blockquote><a href="/state/corsican-republic">Corsican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Morosaglia, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.44,9.3&amp;year=1778" aria-label="Morosaglia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pasquale Paoli"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 3921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 3921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.44,9.3&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
